---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';

const source = `${site.social.github}/blog`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="No cookies, no analytics, no third-party requests — and how to check that for yourself."
>
  <article class="article">
    <header class="article__header">
      <h1 class="article__title">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="page-subtitle">Short version: I don't know you were here.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What this site doesn't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No cookies.</li>
        <li>No analytics, visitor counters, tracking pixels or fingerprinting.</li>
        <li>
          No third-party requests. Fonts, scripts and images are all served from
          this site, so no other company sees your visit.
        </li>
        <li>Nothing is saved in your browser's storage.</li>
        <li>No ads, no comments, no forms, no accounts.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I don't know who reads this site, when, from where, or how many times a
        post has been opened. That's on purpose.
      </p>

      <h2>What still happens</h2>
      <p>
        The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub receives
        your IP address and browser details in order to send you the page, and
        may keep them in its server logs under
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
        GitHub Pages gives site owners no visitor statistics, so I never see
        any of it. The same applies when a feed reader fetches the
        RSS feed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Links to other sites are ordinary links. Once you follow one, that
        site's own rules apply.
      </p>

      <h2>Check it yourself</h2>
      <p>You don't have to take my word for any of this:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open your browser's developer tools and reload the page. In the
          <strong>Network</strong> tab, every request goes to
          <code>{Astro.site!.host}</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          In the <strong>Application</strong> (Chrome) or <strong>Storage</strong>
          (Firefox) tab, cookies and local storage are empty.
        </li>
        <li>
          Read the code. The whole site is open source at
          <a href={source} rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.replace('https://', '')}</a>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How it's built</h2>
      <p>
        Posts are Markdown files, turned into plain HTML by
        <a href="https://astro.build" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astro</a> when the
        site is built. There is no server, no database and nothing running
        behind the pages. Code highlighting and social preview images are also
        made at build time. The text is set in Inter Tight and the wordmark in
        Chakra Petch, both served from this site.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
